pcbkit 1

// RP2040 (Raspberry Pi): Dual Arm Cortex-M0+ MCU, 133 MHz, 264 KB SRAM, external QSPI flash, USB
// 1.1 device/host, 2 PIO blocks (8 state machines), 4-channel 12-bit ADC, 1.8-3.3 V IO, internal
// 1.1 V core regulator
// Compiled from the part sheet `parts/RP2040.json` (`parts.create`); status: checked.
// Datasheet: https://datasheets.raspberrypi.com/rp2040/rp2040-datasheet.pdf (build 2025-02-20
// (3184e62))
// Pins: Raspberry Pi RP2040 datasheet (build 2025-02-20), 1.4 Pinout reference (Tables 1-2) and
// 5.5.2 Pin list (Tables 615-621)
// Footprint: computed per IPC-7351B from the datasheet's package dimensions
// This file may be used, changed and shared without conditions, with the board or alone.

use "W25Q128JVSIQ.pcbkit" as W25Q128JVSIQ

// Note: The exposed pad (57) is the chip's only ground: solder it, with several vias to the ground
// plane.
// Note: The ePad is smaller than common QFN-56 footprints: use the datasheet's 3.2 mm pad, it
// leaves room to route between it and the pins.
// Note: Program memory is external: a QSPI NOR flash (W25Q-series, up to 16 MB) is required.
// Note: The USB bootloader needs a 12 MHz crystal or clock on XIN; USB_DP/USB_DM need 27 ohm
// series resistors, pull-ups are internal.
// Note: TESTEN to GND. RUN low resets; SWCLK/SWDIO to a debug header.
// Note: GPIO26-29 are the ADC inputs (ADC0-3), powered from ADC_AVDD; any GPIO can carry SIO, PIO0
// or PIO1.

/// RP2040 — Dual Arm Cortex-M0+ MCU, 133 MHz, 264 KB SRAM, external QSPI flash, USB 1.1
/// device/host, 2 PIO blocks (8 state machines), 4-channel 12-bit ADC, 1.8-3.3 V IO, internal 1.1 V
